<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cora people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Náayeri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cora, who call themselves Náayeri, are an indigenous people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-del-nayar">Sierra del Nayar</a> in the Mexican state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayarit">Nayarit</a>, speaking a Uto-Aztecan language. From their mountain stronghold at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesa-del-nayar">Mesa del Nayar</a> they resisted Spanish conquest for nearly two centuries, until it fell in 1722. Some 30,000 people speak Cora today, and their festivals combine indigenous and Catholic traditions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cora-people.jpg" alt="Cora people" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Sofus Lumholtz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coras1896Lumholtz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
